¶ numbers in the oblongs and in the lines at the bottom of the Table are the numbers ¶ to the tones of Bosanquet's cycle of 53, in sect. A. art. 27, and sect. E. art. 18, and ¶ their distribution on this keyboard, which was invented for playing them. The small ¶ letters under the numbers at the bottom of each oblong are the transcriptions of one of ¶ Bosanquet's names, of which all are given in sect. A. art. 27, against the number of ¶ note. The stars preceding the numbers shew those which constitute the duodene of C. ¶ notes of all other duodenes stand in the same relative position to their root. The capital ¶ are 48 out of the 56 tones between the dotted and thick lines in the Duodenarium, ¶ E. art. 18, and those following the sign = are other tones differing from them by a ¶ which are purposely identified with them, see sect. E. art. 25. These are the 48 tones ¶ by i\Ir. Bosanquet for his organ, but he names them as at the bottom of each digital. The ¶ thick Roman letters at the top of each oblong are 36 tones of the meantone temperament, ¶ the numbers below them are the cents in the intervals from c to these notes.

¶ keyboard is applicable to any intonation in which all notes, by the neglect ¶ the comma or skhisma, are reduced to one set of Fifths, no matter whether per¶ or imperfect, as the flat Fifths of the meantone temperament. On referring ¶ the plan, we see how the 53 division is placed on the notes. Mr. Bosanquet
